From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 18:32:50 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: name the status bit each RCU reset reports
 through

The reset controller in the RCU takes two cells: it sets the first bit in
RCU_RST_REQ and then polls the second one in RCU_RST_STAT. Every consumer
in this tree passed the same number twice, on the assumption that the
status register mirrors the request register.

It does not. RCU_RST_STAT is a remap of RCU_RST_REQ. A few domains do
report through the bit they are requested with, most report through a
different one, and two positions in the status word are reset causes with
no request bit behind them at all. The vendor's own reset driver carries
the map, one entry per domain with separate request and status masks, and
it disagrees with the mirror assumption for six of the eight lines this
tree drives:

	line			REQ	STAT
	pcie_phy0		12	24
	pcie_phy1		13	11
	gphy_fw "phy2"		29	28
	gphy_fw "phy3"		28	23
	gphy_fw "phy4"		26	22
	gphy_fw "phyF"		31	30

pcie_phy2 (10) and gphy_fw "phy5" (25) do mirror, as do both USB PHYs in
the second bank -- which is why one of the three PCIe PHYs has always come
out of reset without complaint while the other two report a timeout on
every boot.

Nothing about the hardware sequence changes. The request cell was already
right, so the same line was always driven; only the wait afterwards
watched a bit that could not answer. What changes is that the wait can now
succeed, and that a reset which genuinely does not complete will say so
instead of looking like the timeout everybody has learned to ignore.

The GPHY lines are a device tree fix only. Their handles are taken by name
and then dropped: the firmware loader drives RCU_RST_REQ itself and never
asks the framework for a transition, so those five cells are consumed by
the provider's bounds check and by nothing else. They are wrong all the
same, and a loader moved onto the reset framework would inherit exactly
the timeout the PCIe PHYs have.

Bit 31 is the sharpest case of the remap and the one worth spelling out,
because it appears in both registers with different meanings: in
RCU_RST_REQ it is the fifth GPHY's reset line, in RCU_RST_STAT it is the
watchdog reset cause. The fifth GPHY's status is bit 30, so a poll of bit
31 for that PHY would read the watchdog's bit and not the PHY's -- the
opposite way round from the doubt recorded when that cell was added, where
the risk was taken to be the poll landing on the PHY's own bit.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts
index 1d9661c38f16..863ce6fa36f8 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7560.dts
@@ -242,14 +242,15 @@ partition@1500000 {
 
 /*
  * The four LAN GPHYs this board populates. The reset bit list is not
- * contiguous, so every entry is spelled out; the set bit and the status bit
- * are the same number here, hence the doubled cell.
+ * contiguous, so every entry is spelled out, and the status bit each request
+ * bit reports through is a different number again on three of the four -- see
+ * the map on the reset controller in xrx500.dtsi.
  */
 &gphy_fw {
 	phy_id = <0x02 0x03 0x04 0x05>;
-	resets = <&rcu_reset0 29 29>,
-		 <&rcu_reset0 28 28>,
-		 <&rcu_reset0 26 26>,
+	resets = <&rcu_reset0 29 28>,
+		 <&rcu_reset0 28 23>,
+		 <&rcu_reset0 26 22>,
 		 <&rcu_reset0 25 25>;
 	reset-names = "phy2", "phy3", "phy4", "phy5";
 };
diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_grx550.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_grx550.dtsi
index 10b3fa80aa9c..9c6ce5add717 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_grx550.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_grx550.dtsi
@@ -98,17 +98,21 @@ gphy_wan: ethernet-phy@1 {
 
 /*
  * All five GPHYs. The reset bit list is not contiguous, so every entry is
- * spelled out; on this die bit 31 is the fifth PHY's own reset line and not a
- * group master reset, which is how the vendor device tree names it. A PHY
- * without firmware never answers MDIO and probe is all-or-nothing.
+ * spelled out; on this die request bit 31 is the fifth PHY's own reset line
+ * and not a group master reset, which is how the vendor device tree names it.
+ * That PHY reports its status in bit 30, and bit 31 of the status register
+ * belongs to the watchdog instead -- the request bit and the status bit are
+ * different numbers on four of these five lines, see the map on the reset
+ * controller in xrx500.dtsi. A PHY without firmware never answers MDIO and
+ * probe is all-or-nothing.
  */
 &gphy_fw {
 	phy_id = <0x02 0x03 0x04 0x05 0x0F>;
-	resets = <&rcu_reset0 29 29>,
-		 <&rcu_reset0 28 28>,
-		 <&rcu_reset0 26 26>,
+	resets = <&rcu_reset0 29 28>,
+		 <&rcu_reset0 28 23>,
+		 <&rcu_reset0 26 22>,
 		 <&rcu_reset0 25 25>,
-		 <&rcu_reset0 31 31>;
+		 <&rcu_reset0 31 30>;
 	reset-names = "phy2", "phy3", "phy4", "phy5", "phyF";
 };
 
diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
index 03cdc0c07436..989c741444e4 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
@@ -108,8 +108,34 @@ chiptop: syscon@16080000 {
 	/*
 	 * Reset Control Unit. The mainline lantiq,xrx200-reset driver must be
 	 * a syscon child: its reg is a byte offset into the parent regmap,
-	 * not an absolute address. #reset-cells = <2> is [set_bit,
-	 * status_bit], which on this SoC are not always the same number.
+	 * not an absolute address.
+	 *
+	 * #reset-cells = <2> is [set_bit, status_bit]: the driver sets the
+	 * request bit in the first register and then polls the status bit in
+	 * the second one. On this SoC those are usually *different* numbers.
+	 * RCU_RST_STAT is a remap of RCU_RST_REQ, not a mirror of it, so a
+	 * second cell that repeats the first is a bug and not a convention --
+	 * the poll then watches an unrelated bit and can only end in a
+	 * timeout. The per-domain map for the lines this device tree drives,
+	 * in the first bank:
+	 *
+	 *	line				REQ	STAT
+	 *	pcie_phy0			12	24
+	 *	pcie_phy1			13	11
+	 *	pcie_phy2			10	10
+	 *	gphy_fw "phy2"			29	28
+	 *	gphy_fw "phy3"			28	23
+	 *	gphy_fw "phy4"			26	22
+	 *	gphy_fw "phy5"			25	25
+	 *	gphy_fw "phyF" (GRX550 only)	31	30
+	 *
+	 * Two positions in RCU_RST_STAT are reset *causes* rather than line
+	 * statuses, and neither has a request counterpart: bit 31 is the
+	 * watchdog, which the watchdog node below reads through this same
+	 * syscon, and bit 29 is the global software reset that the reboot
+	 * node requests with RCU_RST_REQ bit 30. Bit 31 therefore means one
+	 * thing in the request register -- the fifth GPHY's line -- and
+	 * something else entirely in the status register.
 	 */
 	rcu: rcu@16000000 {
 		compatible = "lantiq,xrx200-rcu", "simple-mfd", "syscon";
@@ -126,8 +152,10 @@ rcu_reset0: reset-controller@10 {
 
 		/*
 		 * Second reset bank. RCU_RST_REQ2 is at 0x48 and
-		 * RCU_RST_STAT2 at 0x24, so a vendor reset id of 5 translates
-		 * to <&rcu_reset1 5 5>.
+		 * RCU_RST_STAT2 at 0x24. Both lines this device tree takes
+		 * from it, the two USB PHYs, are among the domains whose
+		 * status bit does repeat the request bit, so a vendor reset id
+		 * of 5 translates to <&rcu_reset1 5 5>.
 		 */
 		rcu_reset1: reset-controller@48 {
 			compatible = "lantiq,xrx200-reset";
@@ -379,8 +407,10 @@ dma2rx: dma@1c200000 {
 	/*
 	 * Serdes PHYs. Both syscon phandles are mandatory: one carries the
 	 * PHY configuration word and the other the reset. intel,phy-id has no
-	 * fallback — the driver cannot derive it — and the reset cell is
-	 * doubled because the set and status bits differ.
+	 * fallback — the driver cannot derive it — and the second reset
+	 * cell is the status bit the line reports through, which is a
+	 * different number from the request bit on two of the three: see the
+	 * map on the reset controller above.
 	 */
 	pcie_phy0: phy@18500000 {
 		compatible = "intel,grx500-pciephy";
@@ -390,7 +420,7 @@ pcie_phy0: phy@18500000 {
 		intel,syscon = <&chiptop 0x120>;
 		intel,cgu-syscon = <&cgu>;
 		intel,phy-id = <0>;
-		resets = <&rcu_reset0 12 12>;
+		resets = <&rcu_reset0 12 24>;
 		reset-names = "phy";
 		status = "disabled";
 	};
@@ -403,7 +433,7 @@ pcie_phy1: phy@18000000 {
 		intel,syscon = <&chiptop 0x120>;
 		intel,cgu-syscon = <&cgu>;
 		intel,phy-id = <1>;
-		resets = <&rcu_reset0 13 13>;
+		resets = <&rcu_reset0 13 11>;
 		reset-names = "phy";
 		status = "disabled";
 	};
